1. Lambda Expressions

What it is: A concise way to represent a function interface (an interface with only one abstract method).

Example:

List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
names.forEach(name -> System.out.println(name));



---

2. Functional Interfaces

What it is: An interface with a single abstract method, like Runnable or Comparator. New functional interfaces like Consumer, Supplier, and Predicate were introduced in java.util.function.

Example:

@FunctionalInterface
interface MyFunction {
    int add(int a, int b);
}



---

3. Stream API

What it is: A powerful way to process collections and data in a functional style (e.g., filtering, mapping, and reducing).

Example:

List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
numbers.stream()
       .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
       .forEach(System.out::println);



---

4. Default Methods in Interfaces

What it is: Interfaces can now have methods with default implementations, allowing backward compatibility.

Example:

interface Vehicle {
    default void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle is starting...");
    }
}



---

5. Optional Class

What it is: A container for handling null values and avoiding NullPointerException.

Example:

Optional<String> optional = Optional.ofNullable(null);
System.out.println(optional.orElse("Default Value"));



---

6. Date and Time API

What it is: New java.time package replaces the old Date and Calendar classes with a more powerful and easier-to-use API.

Example:

LocalDate date = LocalDate.now();
System.out.println(date);



---

7. Nashorn JavaScript Engine

What it is: A new engine to execute JavaScript code in the JVM.

Example:

ScriptEngineManager manager = new ScriptEngineManager();
ScriptEngine engine = manager.getEngineByName("nashorn");
engine.eval("print('Hello from JavaScript');");



---

8. Method References

What it is: A shorthand for writing lambda expressions referring to methods or constructors.

Example:

List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
names.forEach(System.out::println);



---

9. Collectors

What it is: A utility to process and collect data from streams.

Example:

List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
List<Integer> evenNumbers = numbers.stream()
                                   .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
                                   .collect(Collectors.toList());



---

10. Parallel Streams

What it is: A way to execute streams in parallel to improve performance.

Example:

List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
numbers.parallelStream()
       .forEach(System.out::println);



---

11. Base64 Encoding and Decoding

What it is: A utility to encode and decode strings in Base64 format.

Example:

Base64.Encoder encoder = Base64.getEncoder();
String encoded = encoder.encodeToString("Hello".getBytes());
System.out.println(encoded);



---

12. Static Methods in Interfaces

What it is: Interfaces can now have static methods, similar to utility methods in classes.

Example:

interface Utility {
    static void print() {
        System.out.println("Static Method in Interface");
    }
}
Utility.print();
